<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norman Kirk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norman Kirk was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1972 until his death in office in 1974, leading the Third Labour Government. He pursued a more independent foreign policy, recognising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, withdrawing troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and sending naval frigates to protest against French nuclear tests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moruroa">Moruroa</a> in 1973. His sudden death at the age of 51 shocked the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norman-kirk.jpg" alt="Norman Kirk" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norman_Kirk_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kirk was born in Waimate in South Canterbury to a poor family; his father was a carpenter and his parents were Salvationists. He left school shortly before turning thirteen, when his father lost his job, and worked as a roof-painter's assistant and a stationary engine driver; in 1941 the army found him medically unfit. In 1943 he married Ruth Miller and joined the Labour Party in Kaiapoi, where he built his own house. In 1953, at 30, he became the country's youngest mayor, and at the 1957 election he won the Lyttelton seat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>At the end of 1965 a group of mostly younger MPs helped him unseat Arnold Nordmeyer as party leader. After Labour won the election of 25 November 1972 with a majority of 23 seats, Kirk became prime minister and minister of foreign affairs. He withdrew all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, abolished compulsory military training and widened links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> his government took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> over its Pacific nuclear tests, and in 1973 he sent the frigates HMNZS Canterbury and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a> into the test zone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moruroa">Mururoa</a>. He refused a visit by a South African rugby team because of apartheid.</p><p>At home his government introduced the Domestic Purposes Benefit for sole parents in 1973, began the Ohu scheme of rural communes, made Māori an optional subject in state schools and kept Lake Manapouri from being raised. Rising oil prices and spending brought soaring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, and price controls followed in 1974. In March 1974 the government began the Dawn Raids, police raids on overstayers that primarily targeted Pasifika peoples, and stopped them with an amnesty in April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kirk kept an intense schedule and ignored doctors' advice to rest. By 1974 he had difficulty breathing, eating and sleeping, and an examination on 27 August found an enlarged heart weakened by embolisms. He died of a pulmonary embolism at the Home of Compassion Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> on the evening of 31 August 1974. His biographer Michael Bassett compared the grief that followed to that after the death of M. J. Savage in 1940. After a lying-in-state in Parliament House and services in Wellington and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a>, he was buried at Waimate. Bill Rowling succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norman Kirk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Norman Kirk</b>'s Labour government sent naval frigates in 1973 to protest against French nuclear tests at Mururoa Atoll.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Waimate, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-44.73,171.05&amp;year=1956" aria-label="Waimate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8630 3744 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Norman Kirk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9080 4014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9080 4014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-44.73,171.05&amp;year=1956">What was here in 1956? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
